# THE BACKEND'S DIRECT RUNTIME DEPENDENCIES: what a person edits. requirements.txt is what is installed.
#
#     requirements.in   <- you edit this: the direct dependencies, and a range only where a range is the point
#     requirements.txt  <- pip-compile's output: EVERY package, transitive ones included, pinned to one version
#                          with the sha256 of each file, installed with `pip install --require-hashes`
#
# Why a lock (engineering-04, ops-27). This file was requirements.txt, with fifteen floating `>=` lines
# among its pins and no lock, so a build next month could resolve to different versions from the ones the
# suite passed on, and the audit workflow had to resolve the set itself to know what it was auditing. The
# environment the review ran in had resolved pandas to a major (3.x) the `>=2.0.0` below was never written
# against. Nothing here was pinned tighter; the ranges below are exactly the ones that were in the old
# file, and the lock records what they resolve to today.
#
# TO CHANGE A DEPENDENCY: edit this file, then, from apps/api, on Python 3.11 (the image's version; the
# lock is compiled for it):
#
#     pip install pip-tools
#     pip-compile --generate-hashes --strip-extras --output-file=requirements.txt requirements.in
#     pip-compile --generate-hashes --strip-extras --output-file=requirements-dev.txt requirements-dev.in
#
# and commit all four files together; tests/test_the_backend_installs_from_a_lock.py fails a lock that
# disagrees with this file or with the dev lock. Dependabot understands the pair and opens the same change.
#
# The lock is compiled on LINUX for Python 3.11, the image's platform: a package only a Windows or macOS
# machine would need is not in it, and its hashes cover every file of each version (so the same lock installs
# on an arm64 container). To run the backend on another platform use the container or WSL. Every package in it
# has a binary wheel for Linux x86_64 and Python 3.11, so the slim image needs no compiler (checked on
# 01-10-2026 with `pip download --only-binary=:all:`; the arm64 wheels were not checked).
#
# NOT IN HERE: pytest and anything else only the test suite needs. That is requirements-dev.in, installed by
# CI and never by the Docker image.
